  text: 'take place; don’t assume it’s at a facility that you associate with the company. You will also want to inquire about the interview procedure:\n' +
    '• “How many interviews typically occur before a decision is made?”\n' +
    '• “Who else will be part of the selection process, and what are their roles within the department or company?”\n' +
    '• “What is the timeframe for filling this position, and how many other people are in consideration at this time?”\n' +
    'Ask about the direction the interview will take: “Would you tell me some of the areas we will address on Thursday?” Or “What do you hope to learn about me on Thursday?” The knowledge gained will help you prepare, and will allow you time to bone up on any weak or rusty areas. This is also a good time to establish how long the meeting is expected to last, which will give you some idea of how to pace yourself.\n' +
    'No matter how many questions you get answered in this initial conversation, there will always be something you forgot, so ask: “If I need any additional information before the interview, may I feel free to get back to you?” The interviewer will naturally agree. This allows you to call again to satisfy any curiosity—and it will also enable you to increase rapport. Don’t take too much advantage of it, though: One well-placed phone call that contains two or three considered questions will be appreciated; four or five phone calls will not.\n' +
    'In closing your conversation, take care to ascertain the correct spelling and pronunciation of the interviewer’s name. This shows your concern for',
