  text: 'behave on mine.” To get this insight, an interviewer examines your behavior in general work situations—“Are you comfortable with your accounts receivable skills?”—then looks for examples: “Tell me about a time when you had a problem with an account.”\n' +
    'Behavioral interviewing also looks for balance. If the interviewer is feeling impressed, he will try to temper a positive response with, “Great, now tell me about a time when things didn’t work out so well.” Fortunately, you prepared for this line of questioning when you completed TJD exercises earlier.\n' +
