  screenshot: 'out/B00J08H0QK/pages/page-157.png'
}
{
  index: 150,
  page: 151,
  text: '2. Why you left. You should have an acceptable reason for leaving every job you have held. The following LAMPS acronym identifies acceptable reasons for leaving a company:\n' +
    '• Location: The commute was unreasonably long.\n' +
    '• Advancement: You weren’t able to grow professionally in that position, either because there were others ahead of you or there was no opportunity for growth.\n' +
    '• Money: You were underpaid for your skills and contribution.\n' +
    '• Pride or prestige: You wanted to be with a better company.\n' +
    '• Security: The company was not stable.\n' +
    'For example: “My last company was a family-owned affair. I had gone as far as I was able to go. It just seemed time for me to join a more prestigious company and accept greater challenges.”\n' +
    'Under no circumstances should you badmouth a manager—even if she was a direct descendant of Attila the Hun. Doing so will only raise a red flag in the interviewer’s mind: “Will he be complaining about me like this in a few months?”\n' +
    'This is a “checkbox” question: The interviewer wants to ask the question, check the box, and move on. You get into trouble with too much information. Any answer longer than two short sentences is too long. Use a phrase from the LAMPS acronym above, keep it short and simple, and then shut up; if the interviewer wants more, she will ask.',
  screenshot: 'out/B00J08H0QK/pages/page-151.png'
}
{
  index: 157,
  page: 158,
  text: 'like a breath of fresh air. Your goal is to be honest and forthright without torpedoing your candidacy.\n' +
    'We all share a weakness: staying current with rapid changes in technology. Changes in technology give everyone an ongoing challenge: getting up to speed with the new skills demanded if you are to do your job well. Your answer can address this very issue and in the process still show that you are someone capable of staying on top of things in a rapidly changing workplace.\n' +
    `First talk about these constantly evolving challenges, then follow with examples that show how you are keeping up with technologies that affect your productivity. "I'm currently reading about ..."; "I just attended a weekend workshop ..."; or "I'm signed up for classes at ..."\n` +
