  text: 'Building Stress Into a Sequence of Questions\n' +
    'Sometimes an interviewer will build stress into a sequence of questions. Starting off innocently enough, the questions are layered and sequenced to dig deeper and deeper, but these stress question sequences will hold few surprises for you. Let’s take the simple example of “Can you work under pressure?”\n' +
    'This example will use a reporter’s technique of asking who, what, where, when, why, and how. The technique can be applied to any question you are asked and is frequently used to probe those success stories that sound too good to be true. You’ll find them suddenly tagged on to the simple closed-ended questions as well as to the open-ended ones. They can often start with phrases like: “Share with me,” “Tell me about a time when,” or “I’m interested in finding out about,” followed by a request for specific examples from your work history.\n' +
    'Can you work under pressure?\n' +
    'A simple, closed-ended question that requires just a yes-or-no answer, but you won’t get off so easy.',
  screenshot: 'out/B00J08H0QK/pages/page-230.png'
}
{
  index: 231,
